@model Bastet.Models.ViewModels.AzureReconcileInitialViewModel

<div id="rec-scan-loading" class="text-center py-3 d-none">
    <div class="spinner-border text-primary" role="status">
        <span class="visually-hidden">Scanning Azure...</span>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-2">Comparing Azure against BASTET...</p>
</div>

@* Shown when the scan itself failed. No rows and no delete option are rendered in that case:
   an unanswered question must never look like "everything was deleted". *@
<div id="rec-scan-error" class="alert alert-danger d-none">
    <i class="bi bi-exclamation-triangle-fill me-2"></i>
    <strong>Nothing was checked.</strong>
    <div id="rec-scan-error-message" class="mt-1"></div>
    <hr>
    <p class="mb-0">
        Because Azure could not be read, BASTET cannot tell which resources still exist, so nothing is offered for
        deletion. Fix the connection and scan again.
    </p>
</div>

@* A failed RE-LINK is a different fact from a failed scan, and reusing the panel above asserted
   three things that are all false here: Azure was read, the scan did succeed, and rows may well
   still be offered for deletion on this very screen. This panel says only what happened, and it
   lives outside #rec-scan-content so the automatic re-scan that follows does not wipe it. *@
@* P6. Re-link is the only control that writes AzureResourceId, and O16 removed its TempData
   banner on the argument that the automatic re-scan is the feedback. Nothing checked that the
   re-scan landed, so a re-link that COMMITTED was reported as "Nothing was checked ... Azure could
   not be read" with no mention anywhere that the write happened. This panel is the re-link's own
   success surface, and like its error sibling it lives outside #rec-scan-content so the re-scan
   cannot wipe it. *@
<div id="rec-relink-success" class="alert alert-success d-none">
    <i class="bi bi-check-circle-fill me-2"></i>
    <strong>Re-link saved.</strong>
    <div id="rec-relink-success-message" class="mt-1"></div>
</div>

<div id="rec-relink-error" class="alert alert-danger d-none">
    <i class="bi bi-exclamation-triangle-fill me-2"></i>
    <strong>The re-link did not happen.</strong>
    <div id="rec-relink-error-message" class="mt-1"></div>
    <hr>
    @* Set from what actually happened, in BOTH directions. This sentence used to be hard-coded, so
       when the follow-up scan also failed the panel asserted a re-scan that never ran, directly
       above "Nothing was checked" with no results below. Writing it in one direction only would
       simply invert the lie - after any earlier scan failure it would claim the results could not
       be re-scanned while they demonstrably were - so runScan's two terminal handlers are the sole
       writers and each sets it explicitly. *@
    <p class="mb-0">
        Nothing was changed. <span id="rec-relink-rescan-note"></span>
    </p>
</div>

<div id="rec-scan-warnings" class="alert alert-warning d-none">
    <i class="bi bi-exclamation-triangle-fill me-2"></i>
    <strong>Check this before deleting anything</strong>
    <ul id="rec-scan-warnings-list" class="mb-0 mt-2"></ul>
</div>

<div id="rec-scan-content" class="d-none">
    <div id="rec-nothing-stale" class="alert alert-success d-none">
        <i class="bi bi-check-circle-fill me-2"></i>
        Everything imported from this subscription still exists in Azure. There is nothing to clean up.
    </div>

    <div id="rec-stale-section" class="d-none">
        <div class="alert alert-danger">
            <i class="bi bi-exclamation-triangle-fill me-2"></i>
            <strong>These BASTET subnets no longer match Azure.</strong>
            Deleting one also archives everything beneath it. Rows with a cascade warning will take child subnets or
            host IP assignments with them, including any you created by hand.
        </div>

        <div class="form-check mb-2">
            <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox" id="rec-select-all">
            <label class="form-check-label" for="rec-select-all">
                <strong>Select all</strong>
            </label>
        </div>

        <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table table-sm table-hover align-middle">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col" style="width: 2rem;"></th>
                        <th scope="col">Subnet</th>
                        <th scope="col">Network</th>
                        <th scope="col">Status</th>
                        <th scope="col">Reason</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody id="rec-stale-rows"></tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <div class="d-grid gap-2 mt-3">
            <button id="rec-go-confirm-btn" class="btn btn-danger" disabled>
                Next: Confirm deletion <i class="bi bi-arrow-right"></i>
            </button>
        </div>
    </div>

    @* Drift that cannot be fixed by deleting anything, so it is listed without checkboxes. *@
    <div id="rec-review-section" class="d-none mt-4">
        <h5><i class="bi bi-eye"></i> Needs review</h5>
        <div class="alert alert-secondary">
            Nothing here can be fixed by deleting anything, so these are listed without checkboxes.
            The reason column says what is wrong with each one. BASTET will not change them automatically.
            Where a range has moved to another Azure subnet, a Re-link button repairs the link in place.
        </div>

        <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table table-sm align-middle">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">Subnet</th>
                        <th scope="col">Network</th>
                        <th scope="col">Reason</th>
                        <th scope="col">Action</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody id="rec-review-rows"></tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="d-grid gap-2 mt-3">
        <button id="rec-back-to-subscription-btn" class="btn btn-secondary">
            <i class="bi bi-arrow-left"></i> Back to Subscription
        </button>
    </div>
</div>
